          | Reported on Form 1040. Eligible for the Foreign Earned Income E=
xclusion (FEIE) if you pass physical presence tests.                     |<=
/span></span>
<span class=3D"line"><span>| CR Corporation (S.A. vs. S.R.L.)      | S.A. (=
Sociedad An=C3=B3nima): Requires a board of 3 directors and a comptroller.S=
.R.L. (Limitada): Idiosyncratic to CR, it only requires a Manager. Highly p=
referred by US expats. | Corporate tax from 5% up to 30% on net income. 15%=
 dividend withholding tax when remitting profits to yourself.              =
               | S.R.L. can often be treated as a pass-through entity by th=
e IRS. Both are subject to Controlled Foreign Corp (CFC) and GILTI tax rule=
s. |</span></span>
<span class=3D"line"><span>| US Incorporation (LLC / C-Corp)       | A US e=
ntity owns the CR corporation. Adds a layer of legal shielding.            =
                                                                           =
                  | The CR subsidiary pays standard CR corporate taxes.    =
                                                                           =
          | LLC remains a pass-through. C-Corps face double taxation. Heavy=
 FBAR reporting required.                                                |<=
/span></span>
<span class=3D"line"><span>| Non-Profit / Faith-Based (Asociaci=C3=B3n) | R=
equires 10 founders under CR law. You can draw a reasonable salary for admi=
nistrative work, but profits cannot be distributed.                        =
                       | 0% Income Tax IF granted "Public Utility" status. =
However, Hacienda audits commercial operations strictly. 13% VAT still appl=
ies to inputs. | Must establish 501(c)(3) equivalency to take tax-deductibl=
e US donations.                                                            =
   |</span></span></code></pre></div></div></div></div>
<p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"break-words" node=3D"[object Object]" style=3D"whi=
te-space: pre-wrap;">Note on Incentives: Costa Rica does heavily incentiviz=
e foreign investment, but Law 6990 (Tourist Incentives) usually requires a =
"Declaratoria Tur=C3=ADstica" from the ICT (Tourism Board). This grants pro=
perty tax holidays and duty-free imports on commercial equipment, but it re=
quires significant capital investment minimums and bureaucratic approvals.
3. Regional Cost &amp; Strategy Differences</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto" class=3D"marker:text-secondary">
<li class=3D"break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-norma=
l [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">San Jose &amp; Liberia (Transit Hubs): G=
uests stay 1 to 2 nights before or after flights. Revenue is steady year-ro=
und. Real estate is cheaper, and utilities are moderate because ceiling fan=
s (like those used in your reference hostel) suffice in many rooms.</li>
<li class=3D"break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-norma=
l [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">Tamarindo &amp; Jaco (Party/Surf Hubs): =
Guests stay 3 to 5 nights. You can charge premium bed rates ($20+), and bar=
 revenue is immense. However, real estate is vastly more expensive, and air=
 conditioning is mandatory, which can triple your electricity bill (ICE/CNF=
L).</li>
<li class=3D"break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-norma=
l [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">La Fortuna (Arenal) &amp; Monteverde (Ec=
o Hubs): Real estate is moderately priced. Bed revenue is average, but tour=
 commissions are massive. Hostels here survive by taking 15% to 25% kickbac=
ks on $100 zipline and rafting tours.</li>
</ul>
<ol dir=3D"auto" class=3D"marker:text-secondary" start=3D"4">
<li class=3D"break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-norma=
l [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">The 5 &amp; 10-Year Proforma Projections
These projections assume you buy or lease a property requiring a $72,000 an=
nual mortgage/rent payment. You employ a local manager, two receptionists, =
two cleaners, and one amazing chef. Costa Rican labor law requires paying C=
CSS ("Caja" or social security) which adds roughly 26.5% to all payroll cos=
ts.
Bed rates start at $13/night (blending 8-bed and 4-bed dorms) inflating to =
$15 by Year 5, and $18 by Year 10.
Scenario A: Fully Optimized (With Food, Drinks &amp; Laundry)
Assuming 40% of your guests spend $15 a day on $8 lunches, $12 dinners, and=
 $2 drinks, plus occasional $5 laundry loads.</li>
</ol>
